<article class="tm-article">
  <nav class="tm-breadcrumb"><a href="/hc/en-us">Help Center</a> / Biogas for transport</nav>

  <p>Heavy transport is one of the sectors where it is hardest to switch to electricity. Trucks, ships and machinery require high energy density and long range. Here biogas is a strong solution, because it can be refuelled and driven the same way as fossil gas – but with a far lower climate footprint.</p>

  <h2>CNG and BioLNG – what is the difference?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Bio-CNG</strong> is compressed biomethane, typically used for buses, vans and regional truck transport.</li>
    <li><strong>BioLNG</strong> is liquefied biomethane that is cooled so it takes up less space and provides long range – ideal for long-haul trucks and shipping.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="tm-callout">
    <strong>Ready to use today:</strong> BioLNG can be refuelled on the same infrastructure as fossil LNG and runs in the same engines. The transport industry can therefore cut its CO₂ emissions significantly without waiting for new technology.
  </div>

  <h2>Why is Shell betting on BioLNG?</h2>
  <p>With the acquisition of <a href="/hc/en-us/p/tm-og-nature-energy">Nature Energy</a>, Shell can supply green gas from Danish plants and blend it into its growing LNG network in Europe. This supports the shift from fossil LNG to BioLNG and gives transport customers a concrete path to lower emissions.</p>

  <h2>Benefits for the transport sector</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Significantly lower CO₂ emissions compared with diesel</li>
    <li>Less noise and fewer particulates in cities</li>
    <li>Familiar engine and refuelling technology – no lengthy transition</li>
    <li>Long range that matches heavy and international transport</li>
  </ul>

